;; The published schema for the cpybkc file layout format.
;;
;; This is the machine-readable contract a generator targets and cpybkc's own
;; validator checks a layout against. It is the artifact docs/layout/SPEC.md's
;; "The published schema" specifies; that section is normative and this file is
;; what it describes.
;;
;; It is written in the notation it describes — tagged forms over z5labs/sexpr-go's
;; grammar — because a generator that can emit a layout can already read this, and
;; a diagnostic about the schema and a diagnostic about a layout are then the same
;; kind of thing with the same kind of span on it.
;;
;; The vocabulary is small and closed. Nine tags appear at the top of this file
;; and nowhere in a layout: schema, sort, reference, form, in, arity, layer,
;; argument, child. Everything else here is the name of a layout form, of one of
;; its arguments, or of a value it may hold.
;;
;;   (schema <name> <version>)   what is described, and which version of it
;;   (sort <name> <member> ...)  a name for what may stand in a position
;;   (reference <sort> <form> <argument>)
;;                               a sort whose member is a symbol naming that
;;                               argument of some <form> in the same layout
;;   (form <tag> ...)            one layout form: where it may appear, how many
;;                               times, its positional arguments in order, and
;;                               the children it admits
;;
;; A form carrying (in ...) is reachable from a context: `top-level`, meaning
;; the layout itself, or the name of a sort. A form carrying no (in ...) is a
;; child, reachable only where a parent's (child ...) names it. Arity always
;; sits at the position rather than on the form, which is why `charset` is
;; required under `encoding` and optional under `encoding-override` without
;; being declared twice.
;;
;; Arities are spelled `exactly-one`, `at-most-one`, `one-or-more`,
;; `zero-or-more` and `two-or-more`, for SPEC.md's 1, 0..1, 1..n, 0..n and the
;; two-or-more `seq` and `alt` take. They are symbols rather than SPEC.md's
;; digits because `0..1` is not a lexeme the grammar admits.
;;
;; Argument sorts are a primitive — `symbol`, `text`, `number`,
;; `positive-number` — a sort declared below, or an inline closed set written
;; `(values ...)`. Each closed set is inline because each is used in exactly one
;; position; a set named and referred to by name would be a second place to look
;; for what a `recfm` may say.
;;
;; What this file does not carry, and where each lands instead, is
;; docs/layout/SPEC.md's "What the schema does not say". In short: conditional
;; arities, spellings admitted in order to be rejected by name, rules relating
;; two forms, and everything needing a copybook are the layout reader's and
;; `resolve`'s. A layout this schema accepts is well-formed, not valid.

(schema layout 1)

;;; Sorts --------------------------------------------------------------------

;; An item reference — SPEC.md's "An item reference" — wherever one appears.
(sort item-ref (form item))

;; A run of bytes given literally, with no charset and no padding.
(sort byte-string (form bytes))

;; A discriminator literal: text, a number, or bytes. Which one it is decides
;; what it is compared against, and that resolution is `resolve`'s.
(sort literal text number byte-string)

;; What `when` tests a value against: one literal, or one of several.
(sort match literal (form one-of))

;; What lowers into a predicate: a test on an item's bytes. The set is closed
;; for v1, and it is the whole of what an arm may be selected by — an arm
;; selected by nothing at all would be a default arm, and there is none.
(sort predicate (form equals) (form one-of))

;; A discrimination strategy: a predicate, or the record that carries nothing to
;; test. `single-record-type` lowers into the absence of a predicate rather than
;; into a member of the set above, which is why the two sorts are not one.
(sort strategy predicate (symbol single-record-type))

;; One alternative of a variant, and the predicate selecting it.
(sort variant-arm (form arm))

;; One alternative of a variant, and the occurrences it is taken for. A variant
;; is settled by testing bytes or by position and never by both, which is why
;; this is a sort of its own rather than a second shape of the one above: two
;; forms put the half of that rule that is structural in the schema.
(sort scheduled-arm (form arm))

;; What a rename may name: an item inside a record, or a record itself. A record
;; is admitted here and nowhere else, because a record's top-level item is the
;; one item an item reference cannot reach — the `record` form has already
;; stated it, so a path never repeats it.
(sort rename-target item-ref record-name)

;; A sequencing expression: a record name, or one of the seven operators. The
;; set is closed for the same reason the strategies are.
(sort expression
  record-name
  (form seq)
  (form alt)
  (form *)
  (form +)
  (form ?)
  (form times)
  (form when))

;; A symbol naming a record the same layout defines.
(reference record-name record name)

;;; References and literals --------------------------------------------------

;; (item <record-name> <name> ...) — the record, then the path from its
;; top-level item down to the item named, one name per level, outermost first.
;; The top-level item's own name is not repeated. Each name in the path is a
;; COBOL name, so it is a `data-name`; the record name is the layout's own and
;; stays a symbol.
(form item (in item-ref)
  (argument record record-name exactly-one)
  (argument name data-name one-or-more))

;; (bytes "F0F1") — hexadecimal digits in pairs. That the text is hexadecimal,
;; and that a byte string of no bytes is a diagnostic, are the reader's.
(form bytes (in byte-string)
  (argument hex text exactly-one))

;;; The encoding profile ------------------------------------------------------

(form encoding (in top-level) (arity exactly-one) (layer encoding-profile)
  (child charset exactly-one)
  (child sign-convention exactly-one)
  (child byte-order exactly-one)
  (child float-format exactly-one))

(form encoding-override (in top-level) (arity zero-or-more) (layer encoding-profile)
  (argument item item-ref exactly-one)
  (child charset at-most-one)
  (child sign-convention at-most-one)
  (child byte-order at-most-one)
  (child float-format at-most-one))

;; `charset` takes a symbol and not a closed set. The code pages are
;; `cobol-go`'s `codec/SPEC.md`'s, and one added to that axis would otherwise
;; have to be added here before an adopter could name it. The axis also takes
;; `none`, which is not a code page but the statement that an item's bytes are
;; not characters, and which only `encoding-override` admits — a value admitted
;; in one position and not the other is a condition of the same kind as the
;; conditional arities `framing` carries, and is the reader's.
(form charset
  (argument value symbol exactly-one))

(form sign-convention
  (argument value (values ebcdic ascii-zone-37 translated-ebcdic realia) exactly-one))

(form byte-order
  (argument value (values big-endian little-endian) exactly-one))

(form float-format
  (argument value (values ieee-754 hfp) exactly-one))

;;; Physical framing ----------------------------------------------------------

;; Which children this form requires follows from the `recfm` value: `lrecl` is
;; required under F and FB, `max-segment` under VBS, `delimiter` and `placement`
;; under line-sequential. The table below is the widest form, exactly as
;; SPEC.md's is, and the conditions are the reader's.
(form framing (in top-level) (arity exactly-one) (layer physical-framing)
  (child recfm exactly-one)
  (child lrecl at-most-one)
  (child blksize at-most-one)
  (child blocks at-most-one)
  (child max-segment at-most-one)
  (child delimiter at-most-one)
  (child placement at-most-one))

;; U is a spelling this schema admits and `resolve` rejects by name, so that an
;; adopter who has one is told what is wrong with it rather than left describing
;; their file as something else.
(form recfm
  (argument value (values F FB V VB VBS U line-sequential) exactly-one))

(form lrecl
  (argument value positive-number exactly-one))

(form blksize
  (argument value positive-number exactly-one))

;; `in-stream` is admitted on the same terms as U.
(form blocks
  (argument value (values deblocked in-stream) exactly-one))

(form max-segment
  (argument value positive-number exactly-one))

(form delimiter
  (argument value byte-string exactly-one))

(form placement
  (argument value (values terminator separator optional-terminator) exactly-one))

;;; Record definitions --------------------------------------------------------

(form record (in top-level) (arity one-or-more) (layer record-definitions)
  (argument name symbol exactly-one)
  (argument source record-source exactly-one)
  (child alternative zero-or-more))

;; Where a record's description is held, and the top-level item in it the record
;; is: a copybook, or a run of entries in a definitions file or a block. It is
;; an argument of `record` rather than a child, because a record has exactly one
;; and the two shapes are one position. A source is written inside a `record`
;; and never at the top level.
(sort record-source (form copybook) (form definitions))

(form copybook (in record-source)
  (argument path text exactly-one)
  (argument top-level-name data-name exactly-one))

;; (definitions "<path>" <top-level-name>) names a definitions file, relative to
;; the layout; (definitions <block-name> <top-level-name>) names a
;; `definitions-block` in this layout. Which of the two is decided by the
;; argument's kind and nothing else: text is a path, a symbol is a block name.
;; The top-level name takes the sort `copybook`'s does.
;;
;; A definitions file names no file and holds no block, and a block the same, so
;; a source is one step from the layout and never a chain.
(form definitions (in record-source)
  (argument origin definitions-origin exactly-one)
  (argument top-level-name data-name exactly-one))

(sort definitions-origin text block-name)

;; A symbol naming a `definitions-block` the same layout defines. Block names are
;; a name space of their own: no position admits both a block name and a record
;; name, so a block may share its symbol with a record.
(reference block-name definitions-block name)

;; One alternative of a REDEFINES outside a repeating group, chosen by naming
;; it. How many a record needs — one per redefine the copybook writes outside a
;; repeating group, and none where it writes none — is `resolve`'s, because
;; counting them needs the copybook.
(form alternative
  (argument item item-ref exactly-one))

;; The substitute name is text and is carried verbatim: it is language-neutral,
;; and turning a name into an identifier is a generator's work.
(form rename (in top-level) (arity zero-or-more) (layer record-definitions)
  (argument target rename-target exactly-one)
  (argument name text exactly-one))

(form copybook-reading (in top-level) (arity at-most-one) (layer record-definitions)
  (child occurs-depending-on exactly-one))

(form occurs-depending-on
  (argument value (values odoslide noodoslide) exactly-one))

;;; Definitions ---------------------------------------------------------------

;; A run of a copybook's entries, transcribed: SPEC.md's "Definitions". An entry
;; body is held in a `definitions-block` in a layout, or in a definitions file of
;; its own, and a definitions file is one block's body: the forms its top level
;; holds are exactly what a block holds after its name, checked against these
;; same declarations. There is no second schema and no second version for it.
;;
;; The block's name is the layout's own identifier, never a COBOL name, so it is
;; a symbol and never a data-name. That a name is defined once, and that some
;; record names every block, relate two forms and are the layout reader's.
(form definitions-block (in top-level) (arity zero-or-more) (layer record-definitions)
  (argument name symbol exactly-one)
  (argument entry description-entry one-or-more))

;; What an entry body holds. `entry` is reachable here and nowhere else: it is
;; not a top-level form of a layout, and it is not a child form either, so the
;; tags reserved for children across every layout do not grow.
(sort description-entry (form entry))

;; (entry <level> <name> <clause> ...) — one `cobol-go` DataDescriptionEntry.
;; Entries are flat; the hierarchy is the level numbers', which `copybook.Build`
;; folds. Which levels exist, and which clauses an entry at a level may carry,
;; are `cobol-go`'s to judge rather than this file's to list.
(form entry (in description-entry)
  (argument level positive-number exactly-one)
  (argument name data-name exactly-one)
  (argument clause clause zero-or-more))

;; A COBOL word: a symbol in the case the copybook wrote it, or `(word "...")`
;; where `sexpr-go` would read the spelling as something other than that symbol
;; — today only `nil`. It is the sort of every layout position that names a
;; COBOL item too: an item reference's path, a source's top-level name, an
;; arm's alternative and a `take-alternative`'s. That a `word` is needless
;; around a spelling that reads as a symbol cannot be said here, and is the
;; entry reader's and the layout reader's.
(sort data-name symbol (form word))

(form word (in data-name)
  (argument spelling text exactly-one))

;; A COBOL literal token, delimiters included: "'A'", "-12.50", "50". The sort
;; is not `literal`, which is the discriminator literal's. That the text holds
;; exactly one COBOL token is the entry reader's, because the token is COBOL's.
(sort cobol-literal text)

;; What a value, or either end of a range, may be: literal text or a word.
;; Which of the two a position takes is `cobol-go`'s to judge.
(sort cobol-operand cobol-literal data-name)

;; `through` is one form wherever it appears: a range of values, and the range
;; a RENAMES names.
(sort cobol-range (form through))

(form through (in cobol-range)
  (argument from cobol-operand exactly-one)
  (argument to cobol-operand exactly-one))

(sort value-operand cobol-operand cobol-range)

(sort renamed-items data-name cobol-range)

;; One form per clause kind of `cobol-go`'s data-description grammar, twelve.
;; Every clause and operand form is contextual, declared into a sort. A
;; closed-set operand — a usage, a sign position, a synchronization direction,
;; a key order — is a symbol, and its values are `cobol-go`'s, not a list here.
(sort clause
  (form redefines)
  (form pic)
  (form usage)
  (form value)
  (form occurs)
  (form sign)
  (form synchronized)
  (form justified)
  (form blank-when-zero)
  (form global)
  (form external)
  (form renames))

(form redefines (in clause)
  (argument redefined data-name exactly-one))

(form pic (in clause)
  (argument picture text exactly-one))

(form usage (in clause)
  (argument usage symbol exactly-one))

(form value (in clause)
  (argument operand value-operand one-or-more))

;; The count, then the phrases. That `to`, `depending-on` and `indexed-by`
;; appear at most once each, and that the phrases come in that order with the
;; keys between, count one form against another and are the entry reader's.
(form occurs (in clause)
  (argument count cobol-literal exactly-one)
  (argument phrase occurs-phrase zero-or-more))

(sort occurs-phrase
  (form to)
  (form depending-on)
  (form key)
  (form indexed-by))

(form to (in occurs-phrase)
  (argument bound cobol-literal exactly-one))

(form depending-on (in occurs-phrase)
  (argument item data-name exactly-one))

(form key (in occurs-phrase)
  (argument order symbol exactly-one)
  (argument item data-name exactly-one))

(form indexed-by (in occurs-phrase)
  (argument index data-name exactly-one))

;; SEPARATE is an optional last argument, absent where the copybook wrote none.
(form sign (in clause)
  (argument position symbol exactly-one)
  (argument separate (values SEPARATE) at-most-one))

(form synchronized (in clause)
  (argument direction symbol at-most-one))

;; A clause COBOL writes with no operand is a form with no arguments: its
;; presence is the clause.
(form justified (in clause))

(form blank-when-zero (in clause))

(form global (in clause))

(form external (in clause))

(form renames (in clause)
  (argument renamed renamed-items exactly-one))

;;; Discrimination ------------------------------------------------------------

;; SPEC.md requires exactly one `discriminate` per `record`. This schema states
;; the half of that it can — that a layout carries at least one — because the
;; other half relates two forms and is the reader's.
(form discriminate (in top-level) (arity one-or-more) (layer discrimination)
  (argument record record-name exactly-one)
  (argument strategy strategy exactly-one))

(form equals (in predicate)
  (argument item item-ref exactly-one)
  (argument value literal exactly-one))

(form one-of (in predicate)
  (argument item item-ref exactly-one)
  (argument value literal one-or-more))

;; A redefine inside a repeating group: the alternative is chosen once per
;; occurrence, so it is a variant rather than a set of record types. The
;; argument names the item the copybook redefines; each arm names one
;; alternative and the predicate selecting it.
;;
;; That each arm names an alternative the copybook declares at that position,
;; that the group containing it repeats, and where an arm's target may sit are
;; all `resolve`'s: every one of them needs the copybook.
(form discriminate-variant (in top-level) (arity zero-or-more) (layer discrimination)
  (argument variant item-ref exactly-one)
  (argument arm variant-arm two-or-more))

(form arm (in variant-arm)
  (argument alternative data-name exactly-one)
  (argument predicate predicate exactly-one))

;; A redefine inside a repeating group whose entries carry roles rather than
;; types: no byte of an entry says which alternative it is, and the occurrence's
;; position decides. Each arm names one alternative and the occurrences it is
;; taken for, counted from one.
;;
;; That the schedule covers every occurrence the table can hold exactly once is
;; `resolve`'s, because the number of occurrences is the copybook's. So are the
;; rules this schema shares with `discriminate-variant`. What relates two forms
;; — that no variant is named by both, and that two arms name neither one
;; alternative nor one occurrence — is the layout reader's.
(form schedule-variant (in top-level) (arity zero-or-more) (layer discrimination)
  (argument variant item-ref exactly-one)
  (argument arm scheduled-arm two-or-more))

;; `arm` again, in the position a schedule takes. It is the same tag in a
;; different sort and not the arm above, the way `one-of` is a strategy in one
;; sort and a `when`'s value in another: there is no predicate on it, because a
;; scheduled arm tests nothing.
(form arm (in scheduled-arm)
  (argument alternative data-name exactly-one)
  (argument occurrence positive-number one-or-more))

;; A redefine inside a repeating group every occurrence of which takes one
;; alternative. It is not a variant -- there is nothing to choose between, so
;; nothing is discriminated and nothing is scheduled -- which is why it is a
;; form of its own rather than a `discriminate-variant` carrying one arm: the
;; two-or-more above would otherwise become a condition on which of two shapes
;; was written, and this schema could not state it at all.
;;
;; The argument names the item the copybook redefines; the symbol beside it is
;; the alternative every occurrence takes, which may be the redefined item's own
;; name. That the item is redefined at all, that the group containing it
;; repeats, and that the name is an alternative declared over those bytes are
;; `resolve`'s. That exactly one form names each redefine is the layout
;; reader's, as it is for the two above.
(form take-alternative (in top-level) (arity zero-or-more) (layer discrimination)
  (argument redefine item-ref exactly-one)
  (argument alternative data-name exactly-one))

;;; Sequencing ----------------------------------------------------------------

(form sequence (in top-level) (arity exactly-one) (layer sequencing)
  (argument expression expression exactly-one))

(form seq (in expression)
  (argument subexpression expression two-or-more))

(form alt (in expression)
  (argument subexpression expression two-or-more))

(form * (in expression)
  (argument subexpression expression exactly-one))

(form + (in expression)
  (argument subexpression expression exactly-one))

(form ? (in expression)
  (argument subexpression expression exactly-one))

(form times (in expression)
  (argument subexpression expression exactly-one)
  (argument count item-ref exactly-one))

(form when (in expression)
  (argument item item-ref exactly-one)
  (argument value match exactly-one)
  (argument subexpression expression exactly-one))

;; `one-of` again, in the position `when` tests against. It is the same tag in a
;; different sort and not the strategy above: there is no item reference on it,
;; because `when` has already named the item.
(form one-of (in match)
  (argument value literal one-or-more))
